{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:42554464 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:42554464 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42554464","source":"MED","pmid":"42554464","pmcid":"PMC13556257","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13556257"]},"doi":"10.1128/mbio.00360-26","title":"The FleQ-FleN circuit balances flagellar number against fitness in &lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa&lt;/i&gt;.","authorString":"Migueles-Lozano A, Asp M, Rocha ST, Li J, Fanouraki G, Sun AD, Zhang L, Waldbauer JR, Hong J, Shrivastava A, Yan J, Mukherjee S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Migueles-Lozano A","lastName":"Migueles-Lozano","firstName":"Anali","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Genetics & Cell Biology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Asp M","lastName":"Asp","firstName":"Merrill","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut, USA."},{"affiliation":"Yale Quantitative Biology Institute, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rocha ST","lastName":"Rocha","firstName":"Sofia T","initials":"ST","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Sciences, Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA."},{"affiliation":"Biodesign Institute, Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Li J","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Jiaqi","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, Minnesota, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Fanouraki G","lastName":"Fanouraki","firstName":"Georgia","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Genetics & Cell Biology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun AD","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Aden D","initials":"AD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Genetics & Cell Biology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang L","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Lichun","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of the Geophysical Sciences, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Waldbauer JR","lastName":"Waldbauer","firstName":"Jacob R","initials":"JR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of the Geophysical Sciences, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hong J","lastName":"Hong","firstName":"Jiarong","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7860-2181"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mechanical Engineering, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, Minnesota, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shrivastava A","lastName":"Shrivastava","firstName":"Abhishek","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1598-7509"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Life Sciences, Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA."},{"affiliation":"Biodesign Institute, Arizona State University, Tempe, Arizona, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan J","lastName":"Yan","firstName":"Jing","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular, Cellular and Developmental Biology, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut, USA."},{"affiliation":"Yale Quantitative Biology Institute, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mukherjee S","lastName":"Mukherjee","firstName":"Sampriti","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5509-8776"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Genetics & Cell Biology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."},{"affiliation":"Duchossois Family Institute, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7860-2181"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5509-8776"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1598-7509"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"17","journalIssueId":4259917,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"mBio","title":"mBio","essn":"2150-7511","issn":"2150-7511","isoabbreviation":"mBio","nlmid":"101519231"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0036026","abstractText":"Evolutionary constraints governing flagellar number in bacterial pathogens remain poorly understood. While related <i>Pseudomonas</i> species are hyperflagellated, <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> maintains strict monoflagellation through the FleQ-FleN regulatory circuit. Here, we demonstrate that FleN dosage is essential for maintaining monoflagellation and fitness. Confirming earlier reports, wild-type <i>P. aeruginosa</i> displayed unipolar monoflagellation, whereas ∆<i>fleN</i> mutants developed multiple flagella, frequently more than four, in unipolar or bipolar arrangements. These hyperflagellated cells showed marked fitness defects, including reduced growth, attenuated virulence in a nematode infection model, and a competitive disadvantage in co-culture. The virulence defect reflected hyperflagellation rather than the loss of motility, since a non-flagellated ∆<i>fliC</i> mutant retained full pathogenicity. Notably, ∆<i>fleN</i> cells rapidly evolved suppressor mutations in <i>fleQ</i> that partially restored growth and motility without always restoring monoflagellation. Five independent suppressor alleles mapped to key FleQ domains, four in the AAA+ ATPase domain, and one in the DNA-binding domain, consistent with reduced FleQ activity that rebalances the circuit. Single-cell holographic tracking showed that suppressor strains swim with heterogeneous dynamics, including subpopulations that reach wild-type (WT) speeds, despite carrying multiple flagella. Quantitative proteomics indicated that the fitness burden extends beyond flagellar components, with protein-abundance changes across metabolism, stress responses, and signaling. Conversely, selection under high-viscosity conditions drove wild-type cells to acquire enhanced spreading through a recurrent <i>fleN</i> mutation, <i>fleN</i><sup><i>V178G</i></sup>; multiflagellation is therefore accessible. Together, these findings indicate that the FleQ-FleN circuit balances the flagellar number against fitness, holding <i>P. aeruginosa</i> at a single flagellum while retaining the latent capacity to reach multiflagellated states through single mutations.IMPORTANCEBacterial flagella are extracellular appendages that rotate to propel the cell and enable swimming motility. While some bacteria have multiple flagella, many pathogenic species like <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> have just one. Surprisingly, mutants of <i>P. aeruginosa</i> with multiple flagella performed worse, that is, they grew more slowly, were less infectious in laboratory animals, and were outcompeted by wild-type bacteria. Even when some mutant bacteria evolved compensatory changes, they still struggled compared to single-flagellum bacteria. This reveals an important evolutionary trade-off: while multiple flagella might seem advantageous for movement, having just one flagellum allows the bacteria to grow faster and cause more severe infections. This plasticity likely explains why <i>P. aeruginosa</i> is so successful both in the environment and as a human pathogen.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Genetics & Cell Biology, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Charles H. Revson Foundation","grantId":"(25-19)","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R35 GM147131","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R35GM147131","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grantId":"R35GM150803","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Science Foundation","grantId":"2438891&2438892","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grantId":"R35GM147131","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R35GM150803","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Science Foundation","grantId":"2438891&amp;2438892","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Simons Foundation","grantId":"SFI-LS-ECIAMEE-00006634","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R35 GM150803","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Flagella","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GD","qualifierName":"growth & development"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PY","qualifierName":"pathogenicity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Bacterial Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Trans-Activators","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Virulence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Fitness"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Evolution","Pseudomonas","Virulence","biofilms","Flagellar Gene Regulation","Adaptive Fitness"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"FleQ protein, Pseudomonas aeruginosa","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Bacterial Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Trans-Activators","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/mbio.00360-26"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13556257"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13556257?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-13","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}